{
 "id": "a03",
 "label": "A3",
 "title": "Tagalog idioms",
 "summary": "Thirty everyday idioms with their literal and real meanings and the book's example sentences: bukás ang palád (generous), magsúnog ng kílay (study hard), mahabá ang dilá (gossiper), pagputî ng uwák (never), saling-pusà and more",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what an idiom is and why Tagalog speakers use them",
  "Learn idioms built on parts of the body (hands, bones, blood, tongue, eyebrows)",
  "Learn idioms built on animals (rat, raven, cat, puppy, turtle)",
  "Learn other common idioms such as hulog ng langit and parang palengke",
  "Read each idiom inside the book's example sentence"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What is an idiom?",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "An **idiom** is a group of words that together mean something different from the meanings of the separate words. Idioms often carry a local story or logic, and they add colour, grace and precision to speech and writing."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Every language has its own idioms, and learners often find them hard and avoid using them for fear of being misunderstood. The fix is simply **confidence and practice**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "How to pick up idioms",
     "items": [
      "Listen to Filipinos talking in their own language.",
      "Read Filipino comic strips in the leading newspapers, for example Pol Medina Jr.'s **Pugad Baboy** (literally *Pig's Nest*), which shows Filipino life and society.",
      "Watch Filipino TV variety shows and Tagalog dramas, and listen to radio talk shows.",
      "Write down any unfamiliar words or phrases you hear."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Three parts to each idiom",
     "t": "For each idiom you get the Tagalog, its **literal** meaning (word for word) and its **real** meaning, then one of the book's sentences. Filipinos have thousands of idioms; these are some that make conversation come alive."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "intro",
     "title": "From the introduction"
    }
   ]
  },
